0\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nShort thesis (5\u20137 bullets, same data):\n1. GAAP EPS is structurally lower than adjusted: guided gap $1.53/share ($1.53 \u00d7 137.8M = ~$210M of acquisition amortization + PFAS). Buyers at 19\u00d7 adjusted are paying 22.6\u00d7 real allocative earnings.\n2. Capex $0.220B vs D&A $0.380B: the company only spent 58% of D&A on capex in FY2025 [Actual]. FCF is being flattered by under-maintenance; eventual catch-up capex cuts FCF by $150\u2013200M.\n3. Organic growth is unproven: trailing 5-yr revenue CAGR 3.9% [Actual], consensus FY2026 revenue growth 4.97% [Estimate]. The 9.1% EPS consensus depends on margins expanding every year for 5+ years \u2014 beyond the base rate.\n4. \"Secular growth = 25% of portfolio\" is a management assertion with no audited evidence [Unconfirmed]. Hyperscaler capex is itself cyclical; cooling demand follows compute deployment, which is lumpy.\n5. PFAS liability of $235.3M [Unconfirmed\u2014research brief] is a contingent drain; environmental liabilities in industrials historically exceed initial estimates.\n6. Crowding: 95.19% institutional ownership; consensus Buy with 23% upside priced in. Any guidance cut would create correlated selling.\n7. 24.5\u00d7 trailing GAAP P/E (pack Section 8) vs 12-yr median EBIT margin 14.8%: quality is real, but the multiple embeds no cyclical margin.\nLogic combat (Long vs Short): Long logic strength 6/10; Short logic strength 7/10. Gap = 1 \u2192 within <2 threshold, but the short side wins. Conviction downgraded further.\nAdversarial data: no Hindenburg-style report exists for DOV [Unconfirmed]; the absence of a short-seller report does not validate the long.\nKilled arguments I chose not to rely on: (a) \"DOV always grows through cycle\" \u2014 contradicted by 2015\u201316 revenue decline and 2020 revenue decline; (b) \"Tuck-in M&A record is good\" \u2014 pack holds no divisional ROIC; unproven; (c) \"Data-center AI demand is secular\" \u2014 this was said about fiber optics in 2000.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 10 \u2014 AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n37-point audit result: PASS, with 4 flags:\n\u2022 A. Methodology: PASS \u2014 Engine 1 appropriate; DCF not used alone (multiples + reverse DCF + scenarios included).\n\u2022 B. Valuation: FLAG \u2014 ERP (4.8%) is an [Assumption] and is the largest sensitivity. If market-implied ERP is 3.5%, WACC drops to 8.2%, base IV rises to ~$165 \u2014 still below $201.92. Conclusion robust.\n\u2022 C. Bias: FLAG \u2014 my base case is 28% below the consensus mean PT. Possible anchoring to value discipline; red team confirmed the gap, so bias is acknowledged, not hidden.\n\u2022 D. Consistency: PASS.\n\u2022 E. Narrative: FLAG \u2014 \"25% secular growth\" and \"PFAS $235M\" rest on secondary research; tagged [Unconfirmed].\n\u2022 F. Red Team: PASS \u2014 performed (Section 9); it did not change the conclusion.\n\u2022 G. Data integrity: FLAG \u2014 Q4-2024 net income of $1.436B is unexplained (likely one-time gain/tax benefit; sources confirm a $68.6M divestiture gain in Q3-2024, but that alone doesn't explain $1.436B). I excluded this quarter from 