u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1 Business Understanding (3 sentences):\nDover is a diversified industrial manufacturer of specialized equipment and components for fueling/clean energy, printing/imaging, pumps/process, engineered products, and climate/sustainability technologies. It monetizes an installed base through aftermarket parts, consumables, and service, giving a recurring-revenue stream inside an otherwise cyclical capital-goods model. It operates as a portfolio of niche #1/#2 franchises rather than a single integrated industrial.\nUnderstandability: MEDIUM-HIGH (portfolio structure masks segment drivers; no dimensional data available).\n\nStep 2-2 Moat Score (max 21):\n\u2022 Intangible assets (brand/patents): 2\n\u2022 Switching costs: 3 (installed base, aftermarket lock-in)\n\u2022 Network effects: 0\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 2 (niche scale)\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 1 (fueling/clean energy certifications)\n\u2022 Data moat: 1\nTotal: 11/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT (upper end).\nMoat direction: Stable-to-Widening. Evidence: imaging segment margin expansion (27.7% vs 26.3% prior [Estimate \u2014 FY2025 release]), secular growth platforms 25% of portfolio [Unconfirmed]. Narrowing risk: polymer processing cyclicality, EV transition pressuring fueling equipment.\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch Classification:\nSTALWART with FAST-GROWER sub-platforms. Checkpoints: dividend growth ~4\u20136%/yr (yield 0.99%), EPS growth ~8\u201310% guided, moderate P/E (19.1\u00d7 forward adjusted). Core risk: stalwart should trade 15\u201320\u00d7; currently at the top of that band.\n\nStep 2-4 Financial Strength Scorecard:\n\u2022 Revenue growth: 3-yr CAGR ~1.8% (2022\u201325: 8.508\u21928.093) [Actual]; last-4Q TTM growth ~ +5% [Arithmetic].\n\u2022 Operating margin: TTM 17.0% [Actual]; 12-yr median 14.8% [Actual].\n\u2022 EPS growth: FY2025 GAAP EPS $7.94 vs FY2022 $7.42 \u2192 2.3% CAGR (distorted by one-time items) [Arithmetic].\n\u2022 ROE 14.9% [Aggregator]; ROIC ~12\u201313% [Estimate \u2014 computed in 2.5].\n\u2022 FCF margin: 14.0% TTM (FCF $1.177B / rev $8.421B) [Actual]; conversion OCF/NI > 100%.\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: ~0.87\u00d7 (net debt $1.908B / adj EBITDA ~$2.18B) [Estimate].\n\u2022 Interest coverage: ~9\u201310\u00d7 EBIT (FY2025 EBIT $1.373B / est. interest ~$0.14B) [Estimate].\nJudgment: EXCELLENT (financial strength is the moat-adjacent asset).\n\nStep 2-5 Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u201315):\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 (net \u22120.7% shares/yr, price often above IV)\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 (tuck-ins + portfolio shaping; no major impairment in pack)\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 (capex only 2.7% of revenue; high incremental return)\n4. Mix rationality: 2/3 (buyback + dividend + M&A balanced, but buyback discipline weak)\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (Q2-2026 raised guidance; Q2 beat: adj EPS $2.74 vs $2.72 consensus [Estimate \u2014 Yahoo via research brief])\nInsider bonus: 0 (insider ownership 1.26%; no recent cluster buying recorded).\nTotal: 11/15 \u2192 GOOD.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1 R&D Capitalization: R&D not separately held in pack. For diversified industrial, est. ~2% of revenue [Assumption]. R&D reclassification would lift adjusted NOPAT by ~$0.10\u20130.15B and invested capital by ~$0.5B. Adjusted ROIC impact: ~+0.5pp. Immaterial; not capitalized in DCF.\n\nStep 2.5-2 SBC Treatment: FY2025 SBC $0.044B (0.5% of revenue) [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC = $1.338 \u2212 $0.220 \u2212 $0.044 = $1.074B FY2025 [Arithmetic]; TTM adjusted FCF \u2248 $1.133B.\n\nStep 2.5-3 Operating Lease Capitalization: FY2025 operating leases $0.247B [Actual]. Adjusted net debt = LT noncurrent ($2.621B) + current portion ($0.707B) + finance leases ($0.010B) + operating leases ($0.247B) \u2212 cash ($1.677B) = $1.908B. [Note: I did NOT add short-term borrowings ($0.707B) because it is numerically identical to current portion \u2014 the pack warns these columns may overlap. Vendor total debt $3.575B vs my component sum $3.585B: discrepancy $10M (unresolved but immaterial).]\n\nStep 2.5-4 Brand/Data: Industrial niches; brand value not material to DCF. [Unconfirmed] no Interbrand data.\n\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC: NOPAT \u2248 1.462\u00d70.82 = $1.20B FY2026E [Estimate]; Invested Capital = Equity $7.405B + Total Debt $3.585B \u2212 Cash $1.677B = $9.313B [Arithmetic]. Adjusted ROIC \u2248 12.9% vs WACC ~9.3% \u2192 spread +3.6pp. GAAP ROIC ~12% \u2192 gap < 5pp; no special explainer needed.\n\nStep 2.5-6 Owner Earnings (FY2025): NI $1.094B + D&A $0.380B \u2212 maintenance capex $0.220B \u2212 \u0394WC (\u2212$0.300B release) = $1.554B [Arithmetic]; normalized (zero WC release) \u2248 $1.25B [Assumption]. This $1.25B is my core DCF anchor (Section 3).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3-0 Discount-Rate Derivation:\n\u2022 Risk-free: 4.70% [Actual \u2014 10Y Treasury, FRED/TradingEconomics 2026-08-21]\n\u2022 ERP: 4.8% [Assumption \u2014 blend of historical ~5.0% and current implied ~3.8\u20134.0%]\n\u2022 Beta: 1.10 [Assumption \u2014 vendor beta 1.156, window/benchmark unstated; industry typical 0.95\u20131.15]\n\u2022 Cost of Equity = 4.70 + 1.10\u00d74.80 = 9.98%\n\u2022 Pre-tax Cost of Debt: 5.4% [Assumption \u2014 A-/BBB+ industrial synthetic rating], tax rate 18% [Assumption based on guidance mechanics]\n\u2022 After-tax CoD = 4.43%\n\u2022 Weights: Equity 27.274 / (27.274 + 3.585) = 88.4%; Debt 11.6%\n\u2022 WACC = 0.884\u00d79.98 + 0.116\u00d74.43 = 9.3%\n\u2022 Terminal growth: 2.5% [Assumption \u2014 \u2264 long-run nominal GDP]\n\u2022 Sensitivity: WACC 8.5% \u2192 base IV $157; WACC 9.5% \u2192 base IV $132.\n\nStep 3-1 Explicit Forecast (source-driven):\